Patch 20: disable extended partitions at speed 1, gated by pyramid level.

Standalone. Supersedes patch 0019 -- apply INSTEAD of it, not on top.

Patch 0019 promoted this feature unconditionally and split the bar:

    A1 (4K)     +15.92% / +0.52%   ratio 30.6   FAIL (bar 35)
    A2 (1080p)  +22.75% / +0.60%   ratio 37.9   PASS

A1 needs only a 12.5% BD-rate reduction at constant speed to clear. The gap is
small, and the per-sequence data says exactly where to look for it.

Across the eight A1 sequences the speedup is nearly uniform -- 13.30% to 19.38%,
coefficient of variation 12% -- while the BD-rate cost varies sevenfold, from
-0.17% on Crosswalk to +1.22% on PierSeaSide, CV 89%. The speed benefit is a
property of the encoder. The quality cost is a property of the frame. Any fix
must therefore separate frames rather than turn a threshold down, because a
threshold move scales speed and quality together and the A1 ratio would barely
shift. Arithmetic on the measured per-sequence table makes the size of the prize
concrete: exempting PierSeaSide alone takes A1 from 30.6 to 37.2, and exempting
the worst two takes it to 42.7.

Pyramid level is the separator, and this branch has already measured that it is
the right one. When patch 0006c gated orientation pruning by layer depth, the
marginal ratio of the prunes that gate removed was 12.8 -- far under the bar --
which is a direct measurement that partition prunes on frames low in the pyramid
are the bad ones. The mechanism is not subtle: everything coded afterwards
predicts from those frames, so a lost partition there is paid for repeatedly,
while the same loss on a leaf frame is paid once.

Expected shape of the result: less speedup than 0019, disproportionately less
BD-rate. If pyramid levels >= 3 carry roughly half the encode time and well
under half the propagated damage, A1 lands near 9-11% speedup at 0.2-0.3%
BD-rate, comfortably over 35. That is a projection, not a measurement.

On P12. GPT's patch P12 nominally tested this idea at speed 1 using a
!boosted && !allow_screen_content_tools gate, and reported 1.1-1.5% SLOWER with
a BD-rate of exactly 0.00% on both classes. An unchanged bitstream cannot take
longer to produce, so P12 appears not to have taken effect at all. I previously
read P12 as evidence that conditional gates are expensive and wrote a rule from
it; that reading was wrong, and this idea should be treated as untested rather
than refuted.

AVM_S1_EXT_PART_PYRAMID_MIN is the lowest pyramid level that gets pruned. The
default of 3 matches the boundary this file already uses for pyramid-dependent
decisions. Raising it protects more frames and gives up speed; setting it to 0
reproduces patch 0019 exactly, which makes the fallback free:

    -DAVM_S1_EXT_PART_PYRAMID_MIN=2   prune more
    -DAVM_S1_EXT_PART_PYRAMID_MIN=4   prune less
    -DAVM_S1_EXT_PART_PYRAMID_MIN=0   exactly patch 0019

Two arms at 3 and 4 would bracket the curve for one extra run.

